        treatment: "Cricothyroidotomy is the emergency surgical airway of choice in adults and is relatively contraindicated in children under about 12, where needle cricothyroidotomy and jet insufflation are preferred. Percutaneous tracheostomy is usually performed between the second and third tracheal rings; complications include haemorrhage, tube displacement, subglottic stenosis and, late and catastrophically, tracheo-innominate fistula. Cricoid pressure was traditionally applied during rapid sequence induction to occlude the oesophagus, though its routine use is now debated.",
